Here's a complete example of how to create an input with a clickable search icon on the right side:

## HTML + CSS + JavaScript Solution

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <style>
        .search-container {
            position: relative;
            display: inline-block;
            width: 300px;
        }

        .search-input {
            width: 100%;
            padding: 10px 40px 10px 15px; /* Extra padding on the right for the icon */
            font-size: 16px;
            border: 2px solid #ccc;
            border-radius: 25px;
            outline: none;
            transition: border-color 0.3s;
        }

        .search-input:focus {
            border-color: #007bff;
        }

        .search-icon {
            position: absolute;
            right: 10px;
            top: 50%;
            transform: translateY(-50%);
            cursor: pointer;
            width: 24px;
            height: 24px;
            padding: 5px;
            border-radius: 50%;
            transition: background-color 0.3s;
        }

        .search-icon:hover {
            background-color: #f0f0f0;
        }

        .search-icon:active {
            background-color: #e0e0e0;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="search-container">
        <input 
            type="text" 
            class="search-input" 
            id="searchInput" 
            placeholder="Search..."
        />
        <svg 
            class="search-icon" 
            onclick="handleSearch()"
            xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" 
            viewBox="0 0 24 24" 
            fill="none" 
            stroke="currentColor" 
            stroke-width="2" 
            stroke-linecap="round" 
            stroke-linejoin="round"
        >
            <circle cx="11" cy="11" r="8"></circle>
            <path d="m21 21-4.35-4.35"></path>
        </svg>
    </div>

    <script>
        function handleSearch() {
            const searchValue = document.getElementById('searchInput').value;

            if (searchValue.trim()) {
                console.log('Searching for:', searchValue);
                // Add your search logic here
                alert('Searching for: ' + searchValue);
            } else {
                alert('Please enter something to search');
            }
        }

        // Optional: Allow Enter key to trigger search
        document.getElementById('searchInput').addEventListener('keypress', function(event) {
            if (event.key === 'Enter') {
                handleSearch();
            }
        });
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

## React Component Example

If you're using React, here's a component version:

```jsx
import React, { useState } from 'react';
import './SearchInput.css'; // Assuming you put the CSS in a separate file

const SearchInput = () => {
    const [searchValue, setSearchValue] = useState('');

    const handleSearch = () => {
        if (searchValue.trim()) {
            console.log('Searching for:', searchValue);
            // Add your search logic here
        } else {
            alert('Please enter something to search');
        }
    };

    const handleKeyPress = (e) => {
        if (e.key === 'Enter') {
            handleSearch();
        }
    };

    return (
        <div className="search-container">
            <input
                type="text"
                className="search-input"
                placeholder="Search..."
                value={searchValue}
                onChange={(e) => setSearchValue(e.target.value)}
                onKeyPress={handleKeyPress}
            />
            <svg
                className="search-icon"
                onClick={handleSearch}
                xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
                viewBox="0 0 24 24"
                fill="none"
                stroke="currentColor"
                strokeWidth="2"
                strokeLinecap="round"
                strokeLinejoin="round"
            >
                <circle cx="11" cy="11" r="8"></circle>
                <path d="m21 21-4.35-4.35"></path>
            </svg>
        </div>
    );
};

export default SearchInput;
```

## Alternative with Font Awesome

If you prefer using Font Awesome icons:

```html
<div class="search-container">
    <input type="text" class="search-input" id="searchInput" placeholder="Search...">
    <i class="fas fa-search search-icon" onclick="handleSearch()"></i>
</div>
```

## Key Features:

1. **Positioning**: The container uses `position: relative` and the icon uses `position: absolute` to place it inside the input
2. **Clickability**: The SVG has an `onclick` handler that calls your search function
3. **Visual Feedback**: Hover and active states on the icon
4. **Accessibility**: The icon is properly sized and positioned
5. **Bonus**: Enter key support for better UX

You can customize the styling, icon size, colors, and search function according to your needs!

